.Dd September 5, 2026
.Dt VMM_NESTED 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vmm_nested
.Nd nested virtualization support in the bhyve hypervisor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "hw.vmm.nested.enable=1"
.Pp
.In machine/vmm.h
.In dev/vmm/vmm_dev.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
facility lets
.Xr bhyve 8
expose hardware-assisted virtualization
.Pq Intel VMX or AMD SVM
to a guest, so that a level-1
.Pq L1
guest can act as a hypervisor for its own level-2
.Pq L2
virtual machines.
.Pp
It is controlled by a single host-wide switch,
.Va hw.vmm.nested.enable ,
which is enabled by default on hardware that qualifies.
There is no per-virtual-machine flag: every virtual machine created while
the switch is on is nested-capable.
.Pp
When the switch is on, CPUID leaf 1 advertises VMX on Intel hosts, or
leaf 0x80000001 advertises SVM and leaf 0x8000000A reports the emulated
SVM feature set on AMD hosts.
.Pp
A virtual machine takes its nested-capable state from the switch at the
moment it is created, and keeps it for its lifetime.
Turning the switch off therefore does not change a virtual machine that
is already running; it must be destroyed and created again.
.Pp
The hardware probe deliberately does not test for bare metal.
A guest that has VMX or SVM exposed to it reports the same readiness as a
physical host, which is what allows nesting to recurse beyond one level
.Pq L2 hosting L3 .
.Ss AMD SVM
An L1 hypervisor's
.Dv VMRUN ,
.Dv VMLOAD ,
.Dv VMSAVE ,
.Dv CLGI
and
.Dv STGI
instructions are emulated.
On
.Dv VMRUN
the L1 save area is parked, the L2 save area from the guest VMCB is
loaded into the hardware VMCB together with the union of L0's and
L1's intercepts, and L2 runs until an exit that L1 asked to intercept
.Pq intercept vectors, IOPM or MSRPM .
That exit is then delivered to L1 exactly as hardware would: the L2
state and exit information are written to the guest VMCB, and L1 resumes
after
.Dv VMRUN
with GIF clear.
Exits L1 did not ask for are handled by L0 on L2's behalf.
.Pp
When L1 enables nested paging for its guest, L2 runs under a per-vCPU
shadow of L1's nested page table that L0 fills on demand from L2 nested
page faults and discards when L1 requests a TLB flush or switches
.Dv N_CR3 ;
faults L1's table cannot resolve are reflected to L1 as nested page
faults of its guest.
When L1 runs its guest without nested paging, L2 guest-physical
addresses are L1 guest-physical addresses and L0's own table applies.
The SVM feature leaf advertises nested paging and NRIPS only; AVIC, LBR
virtualization, flush-by-ASID and VMCB clean bits are not offered.
.Ss Intel VMX
.Dv VMXON ,
.Dv VMXOFF ,
.Dv VMPTRLD ,
.Dv VMPTRST ,
.Dv VMCLEAR ,
.Dv VMREAD ,
.Dv VMWRITE ,
.Dv VMCALL ,
.Dv INVEPT
and
.Dv INVVPID
are emulated with architectural operand decoding and the architectural
VMsucceed and VMfail result conventions.
L1's VMCS
.Pq VMCS12
is kept as a private per-vCPU copy that is written back to guest memory
whenever the current VMCS changes; no L1-supplied value is ever written
into the hardware VMCS.
.Pp
.Dv VMLAUNCH
and
.Dv VMRESUME
build a VMCS02 and run L2 on the hardware.
VMCS02's host area is a verbatim copy of L0's, so an L2 exit returns to
the same L0 context as an L1 exit; its control fields start from L0's
known-good configuration, and only the fields that must differ for L2
are overridden.
L2 guest state is loaded from VMCS12 with L0's fixed CR0 and CR4 bits
applied so that VM entry is legal.
.Pp
L2 runs under a per-vCPU shadow EPT
.Pq EPT02
mapping L2 guest-physical addresses to host addresses, filled lazily on
L2 EPT violations by composing L1's EPT with L0's.
A fault L1's EPT cannot resolve is reflected to L1 as an EPT violation of
its guest.
.Pp
Hardware VMCS shadowing is never programmed into any VMCS, so it is not
required in order to advertise nested support: Intel parts that have EPT
and unrestricted guest but no shadowing, such as Ivy Bridge, are
supported.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width "hw.vmm.nested.svm_timer_fastpath"
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.enable
Host-wide switch, default 1.
It can only be set to 1 when
.Va hw.vmm.nested.vmx
or
.Va hw.vmm.nested.svm
reports 1; otherwise the write fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Setting it to 0 disables nested virtualization for virtual machines
created afterwards.
As a
.Xr loader.conf 5
tunable the value is applied after the vendor probe runs.
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.vmx , Va hw.vmm.nested.svm
Read-only vendor probe result, a boolean: 1 when this CPU has the
features the nested paths need, 0 when it does not.
On any given host at most one of the two can report 1.
.El
.Pp
The remaining variables are bring-up and diagnostic aids.
They are not a stable interface and may be removed.
.Bl -tag -width "hw.vmm.nested.svm_timer_fastpath"
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.vmx_l2
Run Intel L2 guests on the hardware, default 1.
Setting it to 0 is a kill switch: L1's
.Dv VMLAUNCH
and
.Dv VMRESUME
then report an architectural VM-entry failure instead, which every L1
hypervisor treats as a fatal error for that L2 guest.
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.l2stats
Read-only L2 exit-reason histogram and injection counters for Intel.
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.svm_debug
Log the first nested-SVM events per vCPU
.Pq VMRUN, exits reflected to L1, L2 page faults
to the console.
Slow; bring-up only.
Default 0.
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.svm_l2inj
Read-only histogram of vectors injected into AMD L2 guests.
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.svm_evtq_defer
Carry every requeued L2 event one extra entry in the queue.
A test knob for the event-requeue path.
Default 0.
.It Va hw.vmm.nested.svm_timer_fastpath
Handle L2 i8254 and ACPI timer port reads in L0 rather than reflecting
them to L1.
Default 1.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vm_create 3 ,
.Xr vmm 4 ,
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr bhyve 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
facility appeared as an experimental addition to
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh BUGS
The feature is experimental.
.Pp
Live migration and snapshots of nested virtual machines are not
supported.
.Pp
Nesting deeper than L2 is permitted by the probe but is exercised far
less than L2, and should be treated as unproven.
.Pp
On Intel, exits that L1 asked to intercept but L0 does not are a later
refinement: L2 currently runs under L0's intercepts.
